# Adic completion of a DVR is a complete DVR

Theorem statement:
`IsDiscreteValuationRing.adicCompletion_isDomain_isDiscreteValuationRing_isAdicComplete`
(`a0e3aec5-3479-5c44-9927-bcd6e6f2d355`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `c8a40646-de82-5693-82eb-fc9693205e2f`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $C$ be a commutative ring which is a domain and a discrete valuation ring, and let $\varpi \in
C$ be an irreducible element. Write $\hat C =$ `AdicCompletion (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal C) C` for
the adic completion of $C$ at its maximal ideal, and let $\iota =$ `algebraMap C` $\hat C$ denote
the canonical map. The theorem asserts the existence of three pieces of data, namely proofs that
$\hat C$ is a domain, that $\hat C$ is a discrete valuation ring, and that $\hat C$ is adically
complete with respect to its own maximal ideal `IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal` $\hat C$ (so the statement
packages these as an existential rather than registering them as instances), together with the
conjunction of three further assertions: first, $\iota(\varpi)$ is irreducible in $\hat C$; second,
for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and every $c \in C$, if $\iota(c)$ lies in the ideal of $\hat C$
generated by $\iota(\varpi)^n$ then already $c$ lies in the ideal of $C$ generated by $\varpi^n$;
third, for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and every $w \in \hat C$ there is $c \in C$ with $w - \iota(c)$
in the ideal generated by $\iota(\varpi)^n$.

This is the standard fact that the completion of a discrete valuation ring at its maximal ideal is
again a discrete valuation ring, complete for its adic topology, with the structure map an isometry
(second clause) with dense image (third clause) and the uniformiser remaining a uniformiser. It is
the tool that allows results formulated for an abstract complete discrete valuation ring to be
applied to the completion of a given one, and is invoked throughout the local study of the node
rings and stalks occurring later.

